{"name":"Walk Me Home","hex":"#496568","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"walk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move on the feet by alternately setting each foot (or pair or group of feet: in the case of some non-human animals with four or more feet) forward, with at least one foot on the ground at all times. Compare run.","To \"walk free\", i.e. to win, or avoid, a criminal court case, particularly when actually guilty."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A trip made by walking.","A distance walked."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/walk"},{"term":"home","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dwelling.\nOne’s own dwelling place; the house or structure in which one lives; especially the house in which one lives with one's family; the habitual abode of one’s family.\n\nThe place (residence, settlement, country, etc.), where a person was born or raised; a childhood or parental home; the home of one’s parents or guardian.","One’s own dwelling place; the house or structure in which one lives; especially the house in which one lives with one's family; the habitual abode of one’s family."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To return to its owner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/home"}],"entries":[{"title":"Walk Me Home (Mandy Moore song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Walk Me Home\" is a song by American singer Mandy Moore from her debut album, So Real (1999), and released as the album's second single in December 4, 1999. The song was featured in the film Center Stage and on the reissue of So Real, I Wanna Be with You (2000). It was written and produced by Tony Battaglia and Shaun Fisher, credited as the production duo the Wasabees. Chuck Taylor from Billboard provided a positive review of the song, describing it as \"the best song Janet Jackson never recorded.\""}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/ba/Walk_Me_Home_first_issue_1999.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walk_Me_Home_(Mandy_Moore_song)"},{"title":"Making the Video","blocks":[{"text":"Making the Video is an MTV show consisting of half-hour episodes chronicling the process of filming various music videos. Usually the director outlines the concept of the video and the show often includes light-hearted and humorous moments. It always concludes with a premiere of the finished video. The show premiered on June 28, 1999, and ended in 2010."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Making_the_Video"},{"title":"Walk Me Home (Pink song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Walk Me Home\" is a song recorded by American singer Pink for her eighth studio album, Hurts 2B Human (2019). The track was announced during an interview on The Ellen DeGeneres Show broadcast on February 6, 2019, and it was released as the lead single from the album on February 20, 2019, by RCA Records. \"Walk Me Home\" was written by Pink, Scott Friedman, and Nate Ruess, while the production was handled by Peter Thomas and Kyle Moorman."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/0/04/Pink_-_Walk_Me_Home.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walk_Me_Home_(Pink_song)"},{"title":"Walking","blocks":[{"text":"Walking is one of the main gaits of terrestrial locomotion among legged animals. Walking is typically slower than running and other gaits. Walking is defined as an \"inverted pendulum\" gait in which the body vaults over the stiff limb or limbs with each step. This applies regardless of the usable number of limbs—even arthropods, with six, eight, or more limbs, walk. In humans, limited research indicates that walking may be associated with reduced risk of diseases and premature death."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Women_walking_in_Hyderabad_street.jpg/330px-Women_walking_in_Hyderabad_street.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walking"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Nate_Ruess%2C_Wilton%27s_Music_Hall%2C_London_%2818511614058%29.jpg/500px-Nate_Ruess%2C_Wilton%27s_Music_Hall%2C_London_%2818511614058%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Nate_Ruess%2C_Wilton%27s_Music_Hall%2C_London_%2818511614058%29.jpg/1280px-Nate_Ruess%2C_Wilton%27s_Music_Hall%2C_London_%2818511614058%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Nate_Ruess%2C_Wilton%27s_Music_Hall%2C_London_%2818511614058%29.jpg/500px-Nate_Ruess%2C_Wilton%27s_Music_Hall%2C_London_%2818511614058%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Walk Me Home\" was co-written by Nate Ruess, who previously collaborated with Pink on \"Just Give Me a Reason\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANate_Ruess%2C_Wilton's_Music_Hall%2C_London_(18511614058).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/1987WorldCupTrials.jpg/330px-1987WorldCupTrials.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/1987WorldCupTrials.jpg/330px-1987WorldCupTrials.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Racewalkers at the World Cup Trials in 1987","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1987WorldCupTrials.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Walking_gait_cycle.png/500px-Walking_gait_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Walking_gait_cycle.png/500px-Walking_gait_cycle.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Walking gait cycle starting with the left leg demonstrated. The loading cycle is where foot pronation naturally occurs.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalking_gait_cycle.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif/330px-Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif/330px-Muybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human walking cycle animation by Eadweard Muybridge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuybridge_human_male_walking_animated.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/CGI_Human_Walk.jpg/500px-CGI_Human_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/CGI_Human_Walk.jpg/500px-CGI_Human_Walk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Computer simulation of a human walk cycle. In this model the head keeps the same level at all times, whereas the hip follows a sine curve.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACGI_Human_Walk.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street%3B_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street%3B_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street%3B_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Gustave_Caillebotte_-_Paris_Street%3B_Rainy_Day_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail